## Propagating Trace Context Through RabbitMQ Headers

The key is to **inject** the W3C trace context into RabbitMQ message headers on publish and **extract** it on consume, using `System.Diagnostics` and OpenTelemetry's propagator.

### Publisher (Service A)

```csharp
using System.Diagnostics;
using OpenTelemetry;
using OpenTelemetry.Context.Propagation;
using RabbitMQ.Client;

private static readonly ActivitySource Source = new("ServiceA.Messaging");
private static readonly TextMapPropagator Propagator = Propagators.DefaultTextMapPropagator;

public void PublishOrder(Order order, IModel channel)
{
    using var activity = Source.StartActivity("OrderEvent send", ActivityKind.Producer);

    var props = channel.CreateBasicProperties();
    props.Headers ??= new Dictionary<string, object>();

    // Inject current trace context into message headers
    Propagator.Inject(
        new PropagationContext(activity!.Context, Baggage.Current),
        props.Headers,
        (headers, key, value) => headers[key] = value);

    var body = JsonSerializer.SerializeToUtf8Bytes(order);
    channel.BasicPublish("orders-exchange", "order.created", props, body);
}
```

### Consumer (Service B)

```csharp
private static readonly ActivitySource Source = new("ServiceB.Messaging");
private static readonly TextMapPropagator Propagator = Propagators.DefaultTextMapPropagator;

public void HandleDelivery(object sender, BasicDeliverEventArgs ea)
{
    // Extract trace context from incoming headers
    var parentContext = Propagator.Extract(
        default,
        ea.BasicProperties.Headers,
        (headers, key) =>
        {
            if (headers != null && headers.TryGetValue(key, out var val))
                return new[] { Encoding.UTF8.GetString((byte[])val) };
            return Enumerable.Empty<string>();
        });

    Baggage.Current = parentContext.Baggage;

    // Start a new activity linked to the extracted parent
    using var activity = Source.StartActivity(
        "OrderEvent receive",
        ActivityKind.Consumer,
        parentContext.ActivityContext);

    // Process the message — all downstream spans are now children
    var order = JsonSerializer.Deserialize<Order>(ea.Body.Span);
    ProcessOrder(order!);
}
```

### OTel SDK Registration (both services)

```csharp
builder.Services.AddOpenTelemetry()
    .WithTracing(t => t
        .AddSource("ServiceA.Messaging")   // or ServiceB.Messaging
        .AddAspNetCoreInstrumentation()
        .AddOtlpExporter());
```

### Why this works

1. **`Propagator.Inject`** writes `traceparent` and `tracestate` headers into the RabbitMQ message properties.
2. **`Propagator.Extract`** reads them back on the consumer side (RabbitMQ stores header values as `byte[]`, hence the UTF-8 decoding).
3. Starting the consumer `Activity` with `parentContext.ActivityContext` makes it a **child span** of the producer, stitching both services into one distributed trace.

### Required packages

```
OpenTelemetry.Extensions.Hosting
OpenTelemetry.Instrumentation.AspNetCore
OpenTelemetry.Exporter.OpenTelemetryProtocol
```

> **Tip:** If you switch to MassTransit or NServiceBus, they do this injection/extraction automatically. With the raw RabbitMQ client, the manual propagation above is required.
